{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785702206,"nonce":"BFEeupjIkdQPS1NpvpOMNjGXgxQ=","message":"**Sequencing DECIDED 2026-08-03 (grill; ADR-0066): this lands BEFORE Depot HA (`6cb4a27`).** Adding replicas to a system where one replica going away dead-letters the runs in flight is not HA.\n\nTwo refinements from the grill:\n\n- **Retry windows are sized as a FRACTION of the step timeout**, e.g. `min(60s, 10% of timeout)`, so the cost is proportional rather than absolute. A one-hour build gets the full 60s and loses 1.7%; a two-minute lint gets 12s and loses the same 1.7% instead of half its life.\n- **The clock split is being done properly rather than worked around** — filed separately. Waiting for infrastructure must not bill the step's execution budget, and the queueing-delay finding justifies it independently of HA.\n\n**The failure principle this implements:** block at step boundaries, never mid-execution, every boundary operation idempotent. Safe because of a structural property — with the eager feed a Pod touches the Depot only at Init (fetch) and at the end (drain), so an outage during a 40-minute build is invisible. Stated honestly in the ADR: waiting is NOT free; the argument is that the alternative is worse (at Init nothing is lost; at drain the work is done and discarding it is the expensive mistake).","files":null}]}